ides some power of 10 (choose a max(\u03b1,\u03b2)). So condition satisfied.\n\nThus essentially M can be any integer of the form (u+1)/v where u is of form 2^{\u03b1}5^{\u03b2} (i.e., divisor of a power of 10) and v is a divisor of u+1 such that there exists a with u \u2264 10^{a} (always true) and also s = v must be within digit bound: v between ceil(10^{a-1}/r) ... but r = 10^{a}/u, so lower bound becomes ceil(10^{a-1} * u / 10^{a}) = ceil(u / 10). Upper bound floor((10^{a} -1) * u / 10^{a}) = floor(u - u/10^{a}) = u - 1 (since u \u2264 10^{a}). So s must satisfy ceil(u/10) \u2264 s \u2264 u-1.\n\nThus v must be a divisor of u+1 lying in [ceil(u/10), u-1].\n\nThus possible M are (u+1)/v where u = 2^{\u03b1}5^{\u03b2}, v divisor of u+1 satisfying ceil(u/10) \u2264 v \u2264 u-1.\n\nNow we can ask: What integers can be expressed like that? Might be all integers greater than 1? Let's test small M.\n\nM=1? Can we have M=1? That would mean C = xy, i.e., x*10^{d(y)} + y = xy. Rearranged: x*10^{d(y)} - xy + y =0 => x(10^{d(y)} - y) + y =0. Since all positive, impossible unless something zero. So M cannot be 1. Minimum M? Example M=2? Let's see if possible. Need (u+1)/v =2 => u+1 =2v => u =2v-1. With u composed of 2s and 5s. Try small v. v must be divisor of u+1 =2v, so v divides 2v obviously. Need u =2v-1. Check v=1 => u=1, but u must be divisor of power of 10, u=1 works (2^0 5^0). Then M= (1+1)/1 =2. Does there exist configuration? u=1 corresponds to t=1, meaning 10^{a}/r =1 => r=10^{a}. Then y = x*r = x*10^{a}. But y must have a digits, while x*10^{a} will have at least a+1 digits unless x=1? If x=1, y=10^{a} which has a+1 digits (since 10^{a} has a+1 digits). So not allowed. So maybe M=2 not attainable.\n\nLet's search systematically small M.\n\nTake a=1 (single-digit y). Then 10^{a}=10. Divisors r of 10: 1,2,5,10.\n\nCase r=1: then y=x. Then condition xy|C reduces to x*10 + x = x(10+1)=11x divides x*10 + x = 11x trivially, M = (10+1)/x = 11/x. Since x is single-digit