en ceil(10^{1}/r) and floor((10^{2}-1)/r). For a=2, lower bound ceil(10/r). For r small, many s.\n\nWe can brute mental: Want M=4. Choose t such that (t+1)/s =4 => t+1 =4s => t =4s-1. Need t divisor of 100. Choose s divisor of t+1 (=4s). So s divides 4s obviously. Need s between ceil(10/r) and floor(99/r). Also r = 100/t.\n\nPick s=5 => t=4*5-1=19 not divisor of 100. s=6 => t=23 no. s=8=> t=31 no. s=10=> t=39 no. s=12=> t=47 no. s=16=> t=63 no. s=20=> t=79 no. s=25=> t=99 not divisor of 100. s=... maybe s=... hmm.\n\nTry a=3 (1000). Need t divisor of 1000. Choose s divisor of t+1. Want (t+1)/s =4 => t+1 =4s => t =4s-1. Need t divisor of 1000. Test s values: s=5 => t=19 not divisor. s=8 => t=31 no. s=10 => t=39 no. s=12 => t=47 no. s=16 => t=63 no. s=20 => t=79 no. s=25 => t=99 no. s=32 => t=127 no. s=40 => t=159 no. s=50 => t=199 no. s=64 => t=255 no. s=80 => t=319 no. s=100 => t=399 no. s=125 => t=499 no. s=160 => t=639 no. s=200 => t=799 no. s=250 => t=999 not divisor of 1000. So maybe M=4 not possible? Let's test directly small examples: try x=1, y=4? Then C=1*10+4=14, xy=4, M=14/4=3.5 not integer. x=2,y=8 => C=2*10+8=28, xy=16, M=28/16=1.75. x=3,y=12 (but y 2 digits) => C=3*100+12=312, xy=36, M=8.666... Not integer. Maybe M=4 not possible. Let's test systematically small combos: Use earlier param: M = (t+1)/s where t divisor of 10^{a}, s divisor of t+1, and s between ceil(t/10) and t-1. For a=2, t divisor of 100. List t values: 1,2,4,5,10,20,25,50,100.\n\nCompute t+1 and its divisors s.\n\nt=1: t+1=2 divisors s=1,2. Need s between ceil(1/10)=1 and 0? Actually t-1=0, so s\u22640 not possible except maybe s=1? But s must be \u2264 t-1 =0, impossible. So t=1 invalid.\n\nt=2: t+1=3 divisors s=1,3. Need s between ceil(2/10)=1 and 1 (t-1=1). So s=1 works. Then M = (3)/1 =3. So M=3 possible (we saw).\n\nt=4: t+1=5 divisors s=1,5. Need s between ceil(4/10)=1 and 3. So s=1 works. M=5.\n\nt=5: t+1=6 divisors s=1,2,3,6. Need s between ceil(5/10)=1 and 4. So s=1,2,3 possible. M values: 6,3,2.\n\nt=10: t+1=11 divisors s=1,11. Need s between ceil(10/10)=1 and 9. So s=1 works. M=11.\n\nt=20: t+1=21 divisors s=1,3,7,21. Need s between ceil(20/10)=2 and 19. So s=3,7 possible. M=7,3.\n\nt=25: t+1=26 divisors s=1,2,13,26. Need s between ceil(25/10)=3 and 24. So s=13 possible. M=2.\n\nt=50: t+1=51 divisors s=1,3,17,51. Need s between ceil(50/10)=5 and 49. So s=17 possible. M=3.\n\nt=100: t+1=101 divisors s=1,101. Need s between ceil(100/10)=10 and 99. So s=101 too big, s=1 too small. No valid s. So t=100 invalid.\n\nThus possible M from a=2 are: 3,5,6,2,11,7, etc. Collect distinct: 2,3,5,6,7,11. Also maybe 4 not present. So M=4 not possible for a=2.\n\nCheck a=3 maybe produce 4. Let's compute t divisor of 1000. Need t such that (t+1)/s =4 => s = (t+1)/4 integer divisor of t+1 and within range. So t+1 must be multiple of 4. t divisor of 1000. Let's list t values quickly: 1,2,4,5,8,10,20,25,40,50,100,125,200,250,500,1000.\n\nCompute t+1 mod 4:\n\nt=1 =>2 not multiple of4.\nt=2=>3 no.\nt=4=>5 no.\nt=5=>6 no.\nt=8=>9 no.\nt=10=>11 no.\nt=20=>21 no.\nt=25=>26 no.\nt=40=>41 no.\nt=50=>51 no.\nt=100=>101 no.\nt=125=>126 divisible by 4? 126/4=31.5 no.\nt=200=>201 no.\nt=250=>251 no.\nt=500=>501 no.\nt=1000=>1001 no.\n\nNone multiples of 4. So M=4 not possible for a=3 either. Could be never possible. Let's test M=4 maybe impossible overall.\n\nConjecture: Possible M are all integers except 1 and powers of 2? Not sure.\n\nLet's compute possible M from enumeration for a up to maybe 4.\n\nBut maybe the set equals all integers of the form (2^{\u03b1}5^{\u03b2}+1)/d where d is a divisor of 2^{\u03b1}5^{\u03b2}+1 and d \u2265 ceil(2^{\u03b1}5^{\u03b2}/10). This is the formal answer.\n\nThe problem likely expects answer: The possible values are precisely the integers of the form (10^{k}/t +1)/s where t,s are positive integers with t|10^{k} and s| (10^{k}/t +1) and s between ceil(10^{k-1}t/10^{k}) and 10^{k-1}t/10^{k}. But maybe they'd accept description: All integers M such that there exist nonnegative integers \u03b1,\u03b2 and a divisor d of 2^{\u03b1}5^{\u03b2}+1 with d between (2^{\u03b1}5^{\u03b2})/10 and 2^{\u03b1}5^{\u03b2}-1, and M = (2^{\u03b1}5^{\u03b2}+1)/d.\n\nSimplify: M can be any integer of the form (u+1)/v where u is of the form 2^{\u03b1}5^{\u03b2} (i.e., divisor of a power of 10) and v is a divisor of u+1 satisfying v \u2265 ceil(u/10). Equivalent to M being any integer that can be expressed as (u+1)/v with u consisting only of prime factors 2 and 5 and v dividing u+1 and not too small.\n\nPerhaps they expect answer: All integers M\u22652 such that M divides some number of the form 10^{k}+1? Not exactly.\n\nLet's attempt to characterize more concretely: Since u = 2^{\u03b1}5^{\u03b2}, u+1 is odd (since u even unless \u03b2=0 and \u03b1=0 gives u=1). Its divisors v are odd. The condition v \u2265 ceil(u/10) roughly says v not too small compared to u. So M = (u+1)/v is at most (u+1)/ceil(u/10) \u2264 about 10. So M bounded? Let's compute maximum M: When v minimal allowed = ceil(u/10). Then M_max \u2248 (u+1)/(u/10) \u2248 10. So M \u2264 10 maybe. Let's test: For u large, v min ~ u/10, giving M\u224810. So maximum possible M is 10? Let's test example achieving near 10: Take u=1 (smallest). Then v must be between ceil(1/10)=1 and 0? Actually u-1=0, so v cannot be >0? Wait condition v \u2264 u-1 =0, contradictory. So u=1 not allowed because need s \u2264 u-1 =0, but s must be at least 1. So u must be at least 2? Let's examine condition again: s must be \u2264 u-1 (since s \u2264 y/r? Actually earlier we had s \u2264 u-1 because y = r s \u2264 10^{a} -1, and r = 10^{a}/u, so y = (10^{a}/u)s \u2264 10^{a} -1 => s \u2264 (10^{a} -1) * u / 10^{a} = u - u/10^{a} < u. So s \u2264 u-1. Lower bound s \u2265 ceil(10^{a-1} * u / 10^{a}) = ceil(u/10). So indeed s between ceil(u/10) and u-1 inclusive.\n\nThus for u=1, lower bound ceil(0.1)=1, upper bound 0, impossible. So u must be at least 2.\n\nNow compute maximal M for each u: M = (u+1)/s, with s minimal = ceil(u/10). So M_max = floor((u+1)/ceil(u/10)). As u grows, ceil(u/10) \u2248 u/10, so M_max \u2248 (u+1)/(u/10) \u2248 10 + small. So maximum M is at most 10, maybe exactly 10 achievable? Let's test u=9? But u must be of form 2^{\u03b1}5^{\u03b2}. Smallest such u\u22652: 2,4,5,8,10,... Let's compute M_max for each:\n\nu=2: ceil(2/10)=1, s_min=1, M_max = (3)/1=3. Achievable? s=1 allowed? Need s \u2264 u-1=1, yes s=1 works. So M=3 possible (example earlier). So max 3.\n\nu=4: ceil(4/10)=1, s_min=1, M_max=5/1=5. Achievable? s=1 \u22643, yes M=5 possible (example earlier). Good.\n\nu=5: ceil(5/10)=1, s_min=1, M_max=6/1=6. Achievable? s=1 \u22644, yes M=6 possible (example earlier). Good.\n\nu=8: ceil(8/10)=1, s_min=1, M_max=9/1=9. Need s \u22647, s=1 works, M=9 possible? Let's test: u=8 corresponds to t=8, a such that 10^{a}=r*u? Wait u = t = 10^{a}/r. For a=2, r=100/8=12.5 not integer, so need a\u22653. Choose a=3, 10^{3}=1000, r=1000/8=125 integer. Then s must be divisor of t+1=9, s between ceil(8/10)=1 and 7. Divisors of 9:1,3,9. s=1 or3 work. M = (9)/1=9 or (9)/3=3. So M=9 possible. Example: r=125, choose s=1 => y = r*s =125, x = y/r =1. Then C = x*10^{3}+y = 1*1000+125=1125, xy=125, M=9. Indeed works.\n\nThus M can reach 9.\n\nu=10: ceil(10/10)=1, s_min=1, M_max=11/1=11. Achievable? s=1 \u22649, yes M=11 possible (example earlier). So M=11.\n\nu=16 (2^4): ceil(16/10)=2, s_min=2, M_max = (17)/2 =8.5 floor 8. Need s divisor of 17 (prime) so s=1 or17, but s must be \u22652, so s=17 not allowed (exceeds u-1=15). So no solution for u=16? Let's test actual existence: a such that 10^{a} divisible by 16. Minimal a=4 (10^4=10000). Then r=10000/16=625. t=u=16. t+1=17 prime. s must be divisor of 17, i.e.,1 or17. Need s between ceil(16/10)=2 and 15. Neither works. So u=16 yields no solutions. So not all u produce M.\n\nThus M values limited.\n\nLet's compute all possible M from enumeration for a up to maybe 4.\n\nWe'll generate list manually.\n\nFor each a, list t divisors of 10^{a}. For each t, compute t+1, its divisors s, filter s in [ceil(t/10), t-1]. Compute M = (t+1)/s.\n\nCollect distinct M.\n\nLet's do a=1: t divisors of 10: 1,2,5,10.\n\nt=1: t+1=2 divisors s=1,2. Need s between ceil(1/10)=1 and 0 -> none.\n\nt=2: t+1=3 s=1,3. Need s between 1 and 1 => s=1 works. M=3.\n\nt=5: t+1=6 s=1,2,3,6. Need s between ceil(5/10)=1 and 4 => s=1,2,3 possible. M=6,3,2.\n\nt=10: t+1=11 s=1,11. Need s between 1 and 9 => s=1 works. M=11.\n\nThus M from a=1: {2,3,6,11}.\n\na=2: t divisors of 100: 1,2,4,5,10,20,25,50,100.\n\nCompute each:\n\nt=1: t+1=2 s=1,2. Need s between ceil(1/10)=1 and 0 => none.\n\nt=2: t+1=3 s=1,3. Need s between 1 and 1 => s=1 => M=3.\n\nt=4: t+1=5 s=1,5. Need s between 1 and 3 => s=1 => M=5.\n\nt=5: t+1=6 s=1,2,3,6. Need s between 1 and 4 => s=1,2,3 => M=6,3,2.\n\nt=10: t+1=11 s=1,11. Need s between 1 and 9 => s=1 => M=11.\n\nt=20: t+1=21 s=1,3,7,21. Need s between ceil(20/10)=2 and 19 => s=3,7 => M=7,3.\n\nt=25: t+1=26 s=1,2,13,26. Need s between ceil(25/10)=3 and 24 => s=13 => M=2.\n\nt=50: t+1=51 s=1,3,17,51. Need s between ceil(50/10)=5 and 49 => s=17 => M=3.\n\nt=100: t+1=101 s=1,101. Need s between 10 and 99 => none.\n\nThus M from a=2: {2,3,5,6,7,11}. Combined with previous set gives {2,3,5,6,7,11}.\n\na=3: t divisors of 1000: 1,2,4,5,8,10,20,25,40,50,100,125,200,250,500,1000.\n\nCompute quickly focusing on new M not already seen.\n\nWe'll compute t+1 and divisors.\n\nI'll make table:\n\nt=1: t+1=2 s=1,2. Need s between ceil(1/10)=1 and 0 => none.\n\nt=2: t+1=3 s=1,3. Need s between 1 and 1 => s=1 => M=3 (already)\n\nt=4: t+1=5 s=1,5. Need s between 1 and 3 => s=1 => M=5 (already)\n\nt=5: t+1=6 s=1,2,3,6. Need s between 1 and 4 => s=1,2,3 => M=6,3,2 (already)\n\nt=8: t+1=9 s=1,3,9. Need s between ceil(8/10)=1 and 7 => s=1,3 => M=9,3 (new M=9)\n\nt=10: t+1=11 s=1,11. Need s between 1 and 9 => s=1 => M=11 (already)\n\nt=20: t+1=21 s=1,3,7,21. Need s between 2 and 19 => s=3,7